ompany''s technical bulletins. ICS Platinum sits above it and is offered through Avery Dennison''s specialist converters and installers; the company describes it as covering materials, labour, remanufacturing and replacement, and as a customised warranty for larger or complex projects such as fleet branding.</p>\n<h3>What both programmes have in common</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>They require the whole system to be from qualifying components. A 3M film printed with an unqualified ink or laminated with another brand''s laminate is outside the programme.</li>\n<li>They require preparation and installation per the manufacturer''s bulletins. 3M''s substrate bulletin says plainly that improperly prepared substrates void any 3M warranty.</li>\n<li>They are tied to shops that are trained or certified on the system, which is why certification matters; our article on <a href=\"/certified-vinyl-wrap-installer-3m-vs-avery/\">what a certified installer really means</a> explains the two programmes.</li>\n<li>They are registered per job. Expect paperwork: film and laminate product numbers and lot numbers, the vehicle, the surfaces covered and the installation date. 3M''s pre-installation inspection record, which documents the vehicle''s condition before film goes on, states that failing to complete and sign it voids the 3M product warranties.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<figure><img src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Paint-Pro-Commercial-Fleet-Wrap-by-Vinyl-Wrap-Toronto-Chevy-Express-and-Dodge-Caravan-Custom-Wraps-1024x538.webp\" alt=\"Printed fleet wraps on a Chevrolet Express van and a Dodge Caravan, the kind of job a finished-graphic vehicle wrap warranty covers\" width=\"1024\" height=\"538\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>A printed fleet wrap built from one manufacturer''s film, ink and laminate is what the finished-graphic programmes are designed to cover.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Layer Three: The Installer''s Workmanship Warranty</h2>\n<p>No manufacturer vehicle wrap warranty covers installation mistakes. Bubbles, lifting edges, visible seams in the wrong place, knife marks in the paint, misaligned panels and stretch marks are workmanship, and only the shop that did the work can warrant them. A workmanship warranty is usually shorter than the film''s rated life and is written by each shop, so read it: what it covers, how long, whether it includes removal and reinstallation labour, and whether it transfers if you sell the vehicle. If a shop cannot show you its workmanship terms in writing, assume there are none.</p>\n\n<h2>What a Vehicle Wrap Warranty Excludes Almost Everywhere</h2>\n<p>The exclusions matter more than the headline term. Across the manufacturers'' published terms, these recur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Damage from road debris and accidents.</strong> 3M''s 2080 warranty terms exclude dents and damage from rocks and road debris. Collision damage is an insurance matter; see our guide to <a href=\"/vehicle-wrap-insurance-accident-coverage/\">vehicle wrap insurance in Ontario</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Abrasion and loss of gloss.</strong> 3M''s print film bulletins state that abrasion damage and loss of gloss are not covered by any 3M warranty.</li>\n<li><strong>Horizontal surfaces beyond their shorter term.</strong> Hoods, roofs and trunk lids carry reduced terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Poor substrate.</strong> Film over rust, chalked paint, fresh paint or aftermarket paint. Avery Dennison states that its films are not warranted over aftermarket automotive paint.</li>\n<li><strong>Paint damage on removal.</strong> 3M''s terms exclude paint pulled during removal; the manufacturers'' position is that sound factory paint releases cleanly and that anything else is the vehicle''s condition, not the film''s.</li>\n<li><strong>Misuse and maintenance.</strong> Pressure washing at edges, harsh chemicals, brush washes and neglected contamination. Our <a href=\"/top-8-maintenance-tips-for-vehicle-wraps/\">maintenance tips</a> describe the care the warranties assume.</li>\n<li><strong>Unqualified components or shops.</strong> The programme simply does not apply.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Why a Cheap Quote Often Has No Real Warranty</h2>\n<p>A low quote frequently gets there, and loses its vehicle wrap warranty, by using a film outside the manufacturer programmes, a laminate from a different system, or no laminate at all on a printed wrap. Each of those saves money and removes the finished-graphic warranty at the same time, leaving only the shop''s own promise. Our article on the <a href=\"/cheap-vinyl-wrap-hidden-costs/\">hidden costs of a cheap vinyl wrap</a> goes through how that plays out, and our <a href=\"/vehicle-wrap-laminate-printed-wraps/\">laminate guide</a> explains why the laminate is part of the warranty question on any printed job.</p>\n\n<h2>Questions That Get You a Straight Answer</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Which exact film, laminate and ink will be used, by product number?</li>\n<li>Is this job registered under a manufacturer''s finished-graphic programme, and which one? Can I see the registration?</li>\n<li>What is the warranty term on the vertical panels, and what is it on the hood and roof?</li>\n<li>What does your workmanship warranty cover, for how long, and does it include labour?</li>\n<li>What will I need to do to keep the warranty valid, and what will void it?</li>\n<li>If a panel fails, who do I call, and what happens next?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Keep the invoice, the registration and the product details with the vehicle''s paperwork. A claim years later depends on them.</p>\n\n<h2>For Fleets: Make the Warranty Part of the Specification</h2>\n<p>On a fleet, the vehicle wrap warranty is a line item in the tender, not an afterthought. Specify the film system by name, require programme registration for every unit, ask for a single workmanship term across the fleet, and record each vehicle''s install date and surfaces. Our articles on <a href=\"/fleet-wrap-installation-certified-installers/\">fleet wrap installation</a> and the <a href=\"/fleet-wrap-replacement-schedule-guide/\">fleet wrap replacement schedule</a> cover the rest of the planning.</p>\n\n<h2>Get a Wrap With a Warranty You Can Read</h2>\n<p>Vinyl Wrap Toronto installs 3M and Avery Dennison films and will tell you, in writing, which film system a quote uses and what is warranted. To talk through a wrap for your car or fleet, <a href=\"/contact/\">request a quote</a> or call <a href=\"tel:416-746-1381\">416-746-1381</a>.</p>\n\n<p><em>Sources: 3M Product Bulletins, Wrap Film Series 2080 and <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/1879697O/3m-product-bulletin-ij180c-en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Controltac Graphic Film IJ180C</a> (basic warranty, MCS and Performance Guarantee references, abrasion exclusion); 3M Instruction Bulletin, <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/2419614O/3m-instruction-bulletin-application-substrate-selection-and-preparation-ra-pdf.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Application: Substrate Selection and Preparation</a>; 3M MCS Warranty application materials; Avery Dennison <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/eu/en/Data-Sheets/DIM/ds-Avery-Dennison-MPI-1105-wrapping-series-EN.pdf.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">MPI 1105 data sheet</a> and ICS programme descriptions.</em></p>\n","style":"text-align:start;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.5em;font-weight:300"}]},{"width":25,"background":"#F8F8F8","padding":"10px 10px 10px 10px","blocks":[{"type":"toc","title":"Table of Contents","box":"background-color:#F8F8F8"},{"type":"form"}]}]}]}]', datetime('now')
